Scott asks for an approximable map \(\mathrm{cond}:T\times D\times D\to D\), where \(T=\{\{0\},\{1\},\{0,1\}\}\), satisfying \(\mathrm{cond}(\mathrm{true},x,y)=x\), \(\mathrm{cond}(\mathrm{false},x,y)=y\), and \(\mathrm{cond}(\bot,x,y)=\bot\), and he proposes to define it on the product of Exercise 3.14 by declaring \(0C\cup 10X\cup 110Y\ \mathrm{cond}\ Z\) when \(0\in C\) and \(X\subseteq Z\), or \(1\in C\) and \(Y\subseteq Z\), or \(0,1\in C\) and \(\Delta\subseteq Z\); he then asks for a similar operator \(T\times D_0\times D_1\to D_0+D_1\) and for \(\mathrm{which}:D_0+D_1\to T\) such that \(\mathrm{cond}(\mathrm{which}(x),\mathrm{in}_0(\mathrm{out}_0(x)),\mathrm{in}_1(\mathrm{out}_1(x)))=x\) for every sum element \(x\). The formal truth domain `TD` is Example 1.2, with neighbourhoods \(\Delta=\{0,1\}\), \(\{0\}\), and \(\{1\}\), `trueElt` the element containing \(\{0\}\), and `falseElt` the element containing \(\{1\}\). The iterated product `prod` represents a neighbourhood as `prodNbhd C (prodNbhd X Y)`, the disjoint-sum form of \(0C\cup 10X\cup 110Y\), with components recovered by `inl⁻¹`, `inl⁻¹ ∘ inr⁻¹`, and `inr⁻¹ ∘ inr⁻¹`. The relation that is actually formalized is `condGuard`: \(C=\{0\}\) and \(X\subseteq Z\), or \(C=\{1\}\) and \(Y\subseteq Z\), or \(C=\Delta\) and \(\Delta_D\subseteq Z\). `cond` makes that relation approximable: the product of the three masters satisfies the master clause, `inter_right` preserves the active clause and intersects the outputs through `condGuard_zero`, `condGuard_one`, or `condGuard_master`, and `mono` shrinks a singleton clause by `Tmem_eq_zero` or `Tmem_eq_one`, while a master clause on the larger input splits into the three neighbourhoods of \(T\) on the smaller input. `cond_true` equates the value with \(x\) by splitting a witnessing product into \(C=\Delta\) or \(C=\{0\}\), rejecting the \(\{1\}\) clause by `one_ne_master` and `zero_ne_one`, pushing the \(\{0\}\) clause up into \(x\), and reading the master clause as